«
@@ PendulumG — a laboratory exercise of mechanics: the acceleration of
@@ gravity g, from a pendulum whose bob is a phone that records its own
@@ swing with its gyroscope.
@@ Level 2: the recording, as for SensorPeriod: an array of rows
@@ [ t ωx ωy ωz ], the time in seconds and the rotation rates in rad/s, or
@@ the name of a CSV file of that form in the data directory.
@@ Level 1: the length L of the pendulum, with its unit, and with its
@@ standard uncertainty if it has one.
@@ SensorPeriod gives the period Treal with its uncertainty of type A. The
@@ mean square of the rotation rate gives the amplitude: for a swing of
@@ amplitude θmax and period T, the mean of ω² is θmax²·(2π/T)²/2. The
@@ period for small amplitudes follows, as in the equation Simple Pendulum
@@ of the Equation Library, T = Treal/(1 + θmax²/16 + 11·θmax⁴/3072), then
@@ g = 4·π²·L/T². The uncertainties of Treal and L are combined; the
@@ amplitude is taken as exact, its correction being small.
@@ Returns Treal, θmax and g, tagged.
@@ Author & design          : Jean Wilson
@@ Co-authors & programming  : Claude & Claude Code (Anthropic)
  → pgdat pglen
  «
    pgdat
    If Dup TypeName "text" == Then Rcl End
    If Dup 1 Get 1 Get TypeName "text" == Then Tail End
    → pgtab
    «
      pgtab ⓁSensorPeriod
      @ The mean square of the rotation rate, all axes together
      pgtab →Col →List
      → pgcols
      «
        0
        2 pgcols Size For pgk
          pgcols pgk Get Dup Dot +
        Next
        pgcols 1 Get Size 1 Get /
      »
      → pgper pgmsq
      «
        @ The amplitude in radians, from the central value of the period
        pgmsq 2 * √ pgper UVAL Range→ Drop * 2 π →Num * /
        → pgth
        «
          pgper "Treal" →Tag
          pgth 180 * π →Num / 1_° * "θmax" →Tag
          @ g, with the period for small amplitudes. The power is written
          @ 2 ^, not Sq: on a value with a unit, Sq would count the
          @ uncertainty of the period as that of two independent factors
          4 π →Num 2 ^ * pglen *
          pgper 1 pgth 2 ^ 16 / + 11 pgth 4 ^ * 3072 / + / 2 ^ /
          1_m/s^2 Convert "g" →Tag
        »
      »
    »
  »
»
